# The TLS terminator obsync requires (AGENTS.md requirement 7). It is a
# DEPLOYMENT component: no code in this repository links, implements, or knows
# about TLS, and nothing here is a dependency of the server or the plugin.
#
# TWO WAYS TO GET A CERTIFICATE, and the choice is only about who vouches for
# it. Obsidian on iOS and Android refuses plain HTTP, so one of them is
# mandatory; nothing else in the deployment changes between them.
#
#   internal (below, the default)  A certificate authority Caddy generates on
#     first start and keeps on the `caddy-data` volume. Needs no port open to
#     the internet, no domain you own, no account with anybody -- a LAN name or
#     a name you resolve yourself is enough. The price is that every device
#     must be told to trust that authority once; README.md, "Any network, no
#     provider", has the five platforms' steps.
#
#   public ACME (the commented block)  A certificate every device already
#     trusts, issued by Let's Encrypt on the strength of reaching this server.
#     Needs a real domain whose DNS points here AND ports 80 and 443 reachable
#     from the internet. Uncomment it, delete the `tls internal` block, and put
#     an address you read on the `tls` line so expiry warnings arrive.
#
#     For a PRIVATE name -- one that resolves only on your LAN or VPN -- the
#     same public authority will issue over the DNS-01 challenge, which is
#     answered by a DNS record rather than by a connection, so nothing here
#     becomes reachable from the internet. Caddy answers DNS-01 only in a build
#     carrying your DNS provider's module, which the image pinned in
#     docker-compose.yml does not have; that route means substituting a
#     different, still digest-pinned, caddy image on purpose.
#
# `{$OBSYNC_HOST}` is the hostname from the environment: the same value the
# compose file gives the server as `OBSYNC_PUBLIC_URL`, so a mismatch between
# the certificate and the URL devices are configured with cannot happen.

{
	# Do not try to add the generated root to the CONTAINER's trust store.
	# Nothing inside this container makes a TLS connection to obsync, the root
	# filesystem is read-only by design, and without this line every start logs
	# an alarming `failed to install root certificate` error for an operation
	# that had no purpose here. The devices are where that root has to be
	# trusted, and README.md says how per platform.
	skip_install_trust
}

{$OBSYNC_HOST} {
	tls internal
	# The server is `obsync:8080` on the compose network and nowhere else: it
	# publishes no port, so this is its only reachable address. Caddy adds
	# X-Forwarded-For, -Proto and -Host, which the server trusts ONLY from
	# `OBSYNC_TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS` -- this network -- and from nowhere else.
	reverse_proxy obsync:8080
}

# Public ACME, for a deployment whose ports 80 and 443 are reachable from the
# internet and whose name resolves publicly:
#
# {$OBSYNC_HOST} {
# 	tls you@example.org
# 	reverse_proxy obsync:8080
# }
